      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <div id="which-parts-fail" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">3. Which Treadmill Parts Fail, and Why</h2>
<p>
            Treadmills fail in a fairly predictable order, and most of the order is driven by friction.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The walking belt and deck.</strong> The belt slides over the deck thousands of times a
            session. Without lubricant, or with a worn belt, friction rises, the motor works harder and the
            belt starts to slip or hesitate. Belts last years with care; our guide to
            <a href="/how-often-should-a-treadmill-belt-be-replaced/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">how often to replace a belt</a>
            has the signs, and our
            <a href="/treadmill-belt-replacement-cost/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">belt replacement cost guide</a>
            the prices. A worn deck often goes with a worn belt; see
            <a href="/treadmill-deck-replacement/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">deck replacement</a>.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The drive belt.</strong> The short belt between the motor and the front roller stretches
            and glazes over time. A slipping drive belt feels like the walking belt hesitating under each
            footstrike. It is cheap and, on most machines, reachable under the motor hood; our
            <a href="/treadmill-drive-belt/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">drive belt guide</a>
            explains how to tell the two belts’ faults apart.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Motor brushes.</strong> Brushed DC motors, common in home treadmills, have carbon brushes
            that wear down. Worn brushes cause a loss of power or intermittent drive and are a small part to
            replace; our
            <a href="/treadmill-motor-brushes/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">motor brushes guide</a>
            covers which motors have them.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The motor control board.</strong> The board that drives the motor is the most common
            expensive failure. It is often a casualty of a strained motor, a power surge or a belt left
            unlubricated, so replacing the board without fixing the cause can mean buying another.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The drive motor.</strong> Motors usually outlast the rest of a home treadmill, but not
            always; our guide to
            <a href="/how-long-do-treadmill-motors-last/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">how long treadmill motors last</a>
            sets out what shortens their life.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Sensors, keys and consoles.</strong> A speed sensor that has slipped out of position, a
            safety key whose magnet has weakened, and consoles with failed buttons or displays are the common
            electrical faults. Our guides to the
            <a href="/treadmill-speed-sensor/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">speed sensor</a>, the
            <a href="/treadmill-safety-key-not-working/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">safety key</a>
            and the <a href="/treadmill-console-not-working/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">console</a>
            help you rule out the cheap fixes first.
          </p>
          <p>
            Also on the list: rollers and their bearings (a rhythmic noise that rises with speed), incline
            motors on machines with powered incline, and power cords and switches. Before you buy any part,
            confirm the diagnosis. A board is a $200 guess if the real fault was a $25 drive belt.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="model-and-serial" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">4. Find Your Model and Serial Number First</h2>
<p>
            Every parts seller, official or not, asks for the model number, and most also need the serial
            number, because one model name can cover several builds. A Life Fitness parts guide from Sportsmith,
            for instance, notes that the walking belt can differ between versions of the same 95T model, and
            FitnessRepairParts lists some Life Fitness parts only for machines whose serial numbers start with
            particular letters. The number on the sticker decides the part.
          </p>
          <p><strong>Where to look, by brand:</strong></p>
          <ul>
            <li>
              <strong>NordicTrack and ProForm.</strong> The front cover of the owner’s manual shows where the
              model and serial number decal is on your machine. On many models it is on the frame, low down at
              the front or rear, or above the motor hood; some sit underneath the machine, so the deck may need
              to be folded or the hood removed to see it. NordicTrack and ProForm model numbers have prefixes
              such as NTL or PFTL, and older Sears-sold units carry numbers beginning 831.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Sole.</strong> The serial number is on a sticker on the frame. If you cannot read it,
              Sole’s support team may be able to identify the machine from the model and purchase date, and
              your order confirmation or receipt may carry it.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Life Fitness.</strong> Life Fitness publishes serial-number decal locations for its
              cardio equipment on its support site. On treadmills it is commonly on the frame underneath;
              Sportsmith says to tip the unit on its side to see it.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Precor.</strong> Precor’s own resources page says the serial number is on a label near
              where the power cord enters the machine and on the frame under the left rear. Several Precor
              manuals put it at the lower front edge of the cover.
            </li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            <strong>Model number, serial number and part number are three different things.</strong> The model
            number identifies the machine; the serial number identifies your build; the part number identifies
            the part. NordicTrack and ProForm manuals include an exploded parts diagram with a “key number” for
            each part, and the manual tells you to quote the model number, the serial number, and the key number
            and description of the part when you order. Write all three down before you contact anyone.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Photograph the sticker.</strong> Stickers fade and peel. Take a photo the day you find it,
            and keep it with the manual. Our guide to
            <a href="/how-to-reset-nordictrack-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">resetting a NordicTrack</a>
            and our <a href="/proform-treadmill-troubleshooting/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">ProForm troubleshooting guide</a>
            both start with the same step.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="oem-vs-aftermarket" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">5. OEM vs Aftermarket Treadmill Parts</h2>
<p>
            An <strong>OEM</strong> part is made or supplied by the treadmill’s maker, the same part your
            machine left the factory with. An <strong>aftermarket</strong> part is made by someone else to fit.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Use OEM for electronics and anything under warranty.</strong> Motor control boards, consoles,
            sensors and motors are matched to a particular machine’s firmware, wiring and motor, and an
            aftermarket board that is “compatible” with a family of machines may not behave identically on
            yours. Makers also tie warranties to their own parts: Precor’s service manual tells technicians to
            use only Precor parts and warns that parts it has not approved can void its limited warranty, and
            Precor gives parts bought from it or its dealers a one-year replacement warranty, without labour.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Aftermarket is often fine for belts and consumables.</strong> Walking belts, drive belts,
            lubricant and universal safety keys are made by specialist suppliers to the original dimensions,
            and a good aftermarket belt can be cheaper than the maker’s and as durable. The catch is matching:
            a walking belt must be the exact length and width, and our
            <a href="/treadmill-belt-size-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">belt size guide</a>
            shows how to measure. A drive belt must match the profile and length, ideally by the maker’s part
            number.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Independent parts sellers often sell OEM parts.</strong> FitnessRepairParts, Sears
            PartsDirect and Sportsmith list parts by the maker’s own part numbers, and Sears PartsDirect titles
            its Sole pages “official Sole treadmill parts”. A third-party seller is not the same thing as an
            aftermarket part. Read the listing: an OEM part carries the maker’s part number and says so.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Watch for superseded parts.</strong> Makers replace part numbers over time. Sears PartsDirect
            notes that a superseded part may ship under a new number and look different from the original. That
            is normal; a part marked “no longer available” with no replacement is the warning sign, because it
            may mean the machine cannot be repaired with new parts.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Lubricant is the exception to “generic is fine”.</strong> Most makers specify 100% silicone,
            and some decks are pre-waxed and must not be oiled at all. Check the manual before you buy; our
            <a href="/best-treadmill-lubricant/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">lubricant guide</a>
            explains how to tell.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="where-to-buy" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">6. Where to Buy Parts, Brand by Brand</h2>
<p>
            Start with the maker if the machine is in warranty, and with the maker or an authorised parts
            seller for electronics. Use independent sellers for availability and price, and Amazon for
            consumables.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>NordicTrack and ProForm (iFIT).</strong> Both brands are made by iFIT, and their owner’s
            manuals direct you to iFIT’s customer service, by the contact details on the manual’s front cover,
            to order replacement parts, with the model number, serial number and the part’s key number from the
            manual’s parts list. Your iFIT account shows your machine’s parts and labour warranty end dates. On
            5 November 2026 nordictrack.com and proform.com would not serve their parts pages to us, so confirm
            the current route through their support sites. For ProForm treadmill parts and NordicTrack treadmill
            parts out of warranty, FitnessRepairParts and Sears PartsDirect list parts by model number and iFIT
            part number, including many for older machines; FitnessRepairParts marks parts that are no longer
            available. Our pages on <a href="/nordictrack/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">NordicTrack</a>
            and <a href="/proform-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">ProForm</a> cover the
            brands’ warranties.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Sole.</strong> Sole’s support page routes replacement parts through its “Service &amp; parts
            request” warranty form and lists a US support line, 866-780-7653, and info@soletreadmills.com.
            Independent sellers including Sears PartsDirect, FitnessRepairParts and Tread Parts, a
            Pennsylvania retailer that concentrates on Sole machines, sell Sole treadmill parts by model and
            year. Sole’s F-series has been sold in several versions under the same names, so the serial number
            matters; our <a href="/sole-fitness/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Sole brand guide</a>
            explains the warranty.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Life Fitness.</strong> The Life Fitness Parts Store, on its support site, takes service and
            warranty parts orders for Life Fitness, <a href="/cybex-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>Cybex</a>, SCIFIT, Hammer Strength and ICG equipment, but Life
            Fitness says it is open only to domestic US commercial customers; phone orders carry a $4.95
            convenience fee. Home owners should contact Life Fitness customer support with the serial number.
            Sportsmith and FitnessRepairParts sell Life Fitness treadmill parts by serial range, including for
            discontinued home models such as the 95T. See our
            <a href="/life-fitness/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Life Fitness guide</a> for the
            current range.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Precor.</strong> Precor sells replacement parts through its dealers and directly, asks for
            the serial number when you buy, and gives parts a one-year replacement warranty with no labour. Its
            technicians can recommend an authorised service provider. Precor treadmill parts also appear at
            Sportsmith and FitnessRepairParts. Our
            <a href="/precor-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Precor guide</a> covers the
            machines.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Amazon.</strong> Best for silicone lubricant, universal safety keys, generic walking belts
            in common sizes and drive belts matched by part number. Check the listing’s dimensions or part
            number against your machine, not just the brand name in the title. Our guide to
            <a href="/best-treadmill-accessories/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill accessories</a>
            covers the non-part add-ons.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>A local repair shop.</strong> Many fitness repair technicians stock common parts and can
            source the rest, and they will confirm the diagnosis before you buy. Our guide to
            <a href="/treadmill-repair-near-me/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">finding treadmill repair near you</a>
            explains what a visit costs.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="warranty-first" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">7. Check the Warranty Before You Buy Anything</h2>
<p>
            Many owners buy a part their warranty would have covered. Before ordering:
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Read your warranty’s parts term, not just the motor term.</strong> Treadmill warranties are
            split: a long or lifetime term on the frame and motor, and much shorter terms on parts, electronics
            and labour. A failed control board in year three may be outside the parts term even though the
            motor is covered for life. Our
            <a href="/treadmill-warranty-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill warranty guide</a>
            explains how the terms divide.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Register, or check your registration.</strong> iFIT shows warranty end dates in your account;
            Sole takes parts requests through a warranty form. Many warranties need proof of purchase, and some
            apply only to the original owner, which matters if you bought the machine used.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Ask before opening the machine.</strong> Some makers want a technician’s diagnosis, or
            photographs, before they send a warranty part. Opening the motor hood is normally expected for
            maintenance, but replacing electronics yourself with non-approved parts can affect the warranty, as
            Precor warns.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Keep the old part.</strong> Some warranty and core-charge arrangements need it back.
            FitnessRepairParts, for one, lists a refundable core charge on some motor control boards, refunded
            when the old board is returned.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="worth-fixing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">8. When the Part Costs More Than the Treadmill Is Worth</h2>
<p>
            A treadmill is worth repairing while the repair costs clearly less than replacing it with something
            as good. The arithmetic is simple; the inputs take a few minutes to find.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>1. Price the whole repair.</strong> The part, plus shipping, plus labour if you are not doing
            it yourself. A technician’s call-out typically runs around $75 to $150 before labour and the part,
            as our repair-cost guides explain, so a $200 board can be a $400 to $500 repair.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>2. Price the replacement.</strong> Not the original price, but what an equivalent machine
            costs today, new or used. Our guides to
            <a href="/how-much-does-a-treadmill-cost/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">what treadmills cost</a>
            and <a href="/where-to-buy-a-used-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">buying a used treadmill</a>
            give the ranges.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>3. Weigh the age and what else is worn.</strong> A machine needing a board and a belt in the
            same year, on a motor out of warranty, is telling you something. A machine needing only a drive belt
            is not.
          </p>
          <p>A rough rule we use:</p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Always worth it:</strong> keys, lubricant, drive belts, brushes, sensor adjustments. They cost little and often fix the whole problem.</li>
            <li><strong>Usually worth it:</strong> a walking belt, or belt and deck, on a well-built machine with a sound motor.</li>
            <li><strong>Think hard:</strong> a motor, control board or console on a budget folding treadmill more than five years old, where the repair approaches half the price of a comparable new machine.</li>
            <li><strong>Not worth it:</strong> any repair where the part is listed as no longer available with no replacement, or where the total exceeds the cost of a comparable used machine.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            <a href="/best-budget-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>Budget treadmills</a> hit the “think hard” line fastest, because their electronics cost much the same as a
            mid-range machine’s while the machine itself is worth far less. A mid-range or commercial machine with
            a heavy frame is often worth a board or a motor, especially as its frame and motor warranties may
            still run.
          </p>
          <p>
            If it is not worth fixing, a working-but-tired treadmill may still be worth something to a buyer who
            repairs them, and a dead one has to be recycled properly; our guide to
            <a href="/how-to-dispose-of-a-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">disposing of a treadmill</a>
            covers the options.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="diy-or-technician" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">9. Fit It Yourself or Call a Technician?</h2>
<p>
            Most owners can fit a safety key, apply lubricant and adjust or replace a drive belt with basic
            tools. A walking belt is a bigger job but a manageable one; our guide to
            <a href="/how-to-replace-treadmill-belt/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">replacing a treadmill belt</a>
            walks through it. Motor control boards and consoles are plug-in parts on many machines, but you are
            working inside the motor compartment with mains wiring.
          </p>
          <p>
            Before any job: unplug the treadmill at the wall, not just switch it off, and give the electronics a few
            minutes before touching the board. Photograph every connector before you disconnect it.
            Keep screws in labelled bags. If you are replacing a board because it failed, find out why it failed;
            a dragging belt or a failing motor will kill the new one too.
          </p>
          <p>
            Call a technician for a drive motor, for any fault you cannot diagnose, for anything under warranty
            that the maker wants a technician to handle, and whenever you are not comfortable with mains
            electricity. A good technician also tells you when a repair is not worth it, which can save the
            cost of the part.
          </p>
          <p>
            Prevention is cheaper than either. Most expensive failures start with friction: keep the belt
            lubricated, tensioned and centred, and the motor compartment clean. Our
            <a href="/treadmill-maintenance-checklist/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill maintenance checklist</a>
            sets out the routine.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">10. Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Where can I buy treadmill parts?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                From the treadmill’s maker for electronics and anything under warranty; from independent parts sellers such as FitnessRepairParts, Sears PartsDirect and Sportsmith, which list parts by the maker’s part numbers; and from Amazon for lubricant, universal safety keys and belts matched by size or part number. A local repair technician can also source parts and confirm the diagnosis.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Where do I find my treadmill’s model and serial number?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                On a decal on the frame. NordicTrack and ProForm manuals show the decal’s location on their front cover; it is often low on the frame at the front or rear. Life Fitness treadmills usually carry it on the frame underneath, and Precor says its label is near the power cord entry and under the left rear of the frame. Photograph it once you find it.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Where do I buy NordicTrack or ProForm treadmill parts?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Both are iFIT brands, and their manuals direct you to iFIT customer service to order parts, quoting the model number, serial number and the part’s key number from the manual’s parts list. Your iFIT account shows your warranty end dates. Out of warranty, FitnessRepairParts and Sears PartsDirect list many NordicTrack and ProForm parts by model and part number.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are aftermarket treadmill parts any good?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For belts, drive belts, lubricant and safety keys, a good aftermarket part made to the original dimensions is usually fine. For motor control boards, consoles, sensors and motors, use the maker’s part: they are matched to your machine, and some makers, Precor among them, say non-approved parts can void the warranty.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can home owners buy Life Fitness treadmill parts?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not through the Life Fitness Parts Store, which Life Fitness says is open only to domestic US commercial customers. Home owners should contact Life Fitness customer support with the serial number, or buy from independent sellers such as Sportsmith and FitnessRepairParts, which list Life Fitness parts by model and serial range.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is it worth replacing a treadmill motor or control board?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                On a well-built machine whose frame and belt are sound, often yes. On a budget folding treadmill several years old, a motor or board can approach half the price of a comparable new machine once labour is added, and that is the point to compare it with replacing the treadmill. Rule out cheap causes such as the drive belt, brushes and lubrication first.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">Common Mistakes Buying Treadmill Parts</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">Each of these turns a cheap repair into an expensive one, or a working treadmill into a box of returned parts.</p>
          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ordering by model name instead of serial number</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">The same model name can cover several builds with different belts, boards and motors. Find the model and serial number on the frame, and order by those plus the maker’s part or key number. A part that fits “the 1750” may not fit yours.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying the expensive part before the cheap diagnosis</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">A belt that hesitates may be a $25 drive belt or a dry deck, not a $200 control board. Work through the cheap causes first, or pay a technician to diagnose before you order an expensive electronic part that is often non-returnable.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Paying for a part the warranty covers</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Parts terms are shorter than motor and frame terms, but they are often longer than owners think. Check your warranty and registration before buying anything, and keep the old part in case the maker or seller wants it back.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Replacing a board without fixing what killed it</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Control boards often fail because the motor was straining against a dry or worn belt. Fit a new board without fixing the friction, and it can fail again. Lubricate, check the belt and deck, and check the motor before you power up a new board.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

